In the Wings
The big top can be entered to the north and exited to the south. To the northeast, the grandstand begins its precipitous rise.

As you exhale a sigh of relief, a smallish and hairy animal inexplicably plops down upon you from the upper reaches of the big top.

>south
Connection
This area of matted-down crabgrass lies between the vaulted big top entrance to the north and the enticements of the midway to the east, where a sagging banner hangs crookedly above a turnstile. There is a drinking fountain near the side wall of the tent. 

Stretching away from a small concession stand is a long line of suckers.

The monkey on your back breathes its jungle breath down your neck.

>stand in line
You are now standing at the tail end of the long line.

>wait
Time passes...

>wait
Time passes...

Right next to the long line a much shorter line begins to form.

>get out of long line
You get out of the long line.

>stand in short line
You are now standing at the tail end of the short line.

>wait
Time passes...

The face of the man ahead of you lights up as he spots something. "Hey, guys! It's ME, Jerry," he yells to a sizable group nearby, and they approach.

>wait
Time passes...

"Haven't seen you turkeys in years. Howda hell are you guys?" They all reintroduce themselves. "Hey -- you clowns thirsty? Get in here, I'll buy y'all beer."

"You sure it's not a problem?" asks the third baseman.

"Heck no, just scoot in right here."

With both your resolve and your heaving bosom firm against the crush of interlopers, you are nevertheless forced to backpedal.

>get out of long line
You hear an inner voice whisper, "Do I really want to forfeit my position in the long line?" To which you answer:

>yes
You nonchalantly walk away from the long line.

>stand in long line
A lot of other people must not have had the same idea as you, as they defect nearly en masse over to the short line. Steaming to the front of the line, you get a two-dollar-and-25-cent frozen banana pushed at you and are whisked to the side before you can even count your change.

The monkey on your back vocalizes excitedly in your ear.

>bite the banana
As the monkey on your back catches a whiff of the tangy fruit beneath the chocolate, you feel its primal heart begin to race and, on the back of your neck, its warm breath quicken.

The monkey on your back idly grooms areas of your scalp.

>drop banana
Following its jungle appetite, the monkey dismounts your back, pounces on the banana, and saunters away with it.

>north
In the Wings

One lone hawker stands waiting in the wings, taking his break.

>ask the hawker about candy
"Hey, you're the one I passed the granola bar to in the grandstand. I know that's not what you ordered, but that's all I had left. Well, the guy that was sitting next to you is holding the granola for you."

>up
You forge ahead and up the steps.

